WEBVTT

00:00:00.554 --> 00:00:21.722
<v Casey>Hey there. On today's episode, a surveillance camera company leaks one point six million images from a single misconfigured device, our own MLA takes over the Conservatives right as forestry policy hits crisis mode, and the Deep Dive asks who actually gets to argue with a satellite when a mill curtails and the cruisers who knew the land get laid off. That and more, coming right up.

00:00:35.197 --> 00:00:55.581
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Tuesday, September 22nd, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Working Lands &amp; Industry.

00:00:56.581 --> 00:01:29.365
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're looking at the resource economy today — forestry, ranching, mining — and who captures the value when the technology sitting on top of that land changes. Quick weather note before we get into it: clear skies out at Horsefly Lake this morning, around 8 degrees, high of 18 today, with cloud building through the afternoon and only about an 8 percent chance of rain. 100 Mile House, Williams Lake, and Quesnel are all sitting around 6 to 8 degrees with clear skies this morning, and Redstone's the cool spot at 4.

00:01:29.811 --> 00:01:33.131
<v Casey>No driving advisories out there today, at least none we're seeing.

00:01:33.331 --> 00:01:34.507
<v Riley>Good. Let's get into it.

00:01:34.707 --> 00:01:35.419
<v Casey>Let's get into it.

00:01:36.819 --> 00:01:45.843
<v Casey>A fair amount of today's news is about who holds authority — over a political party, over a highway safety system, over a surveillance camera nobody quite trusts.

00:01:47.043 --> 00:02:13.411
<v Riley>Starting close to home, with a sentencing out of Quesnel. Patrick Russell, 38, pleaded guilty to assault with a weapon, mischief under five thousand dollars, and breach of a release order. RCMP say he tried to force his way into a temporary shelter by breaking the windows with a hammer, attempting to get at someone using the services inside. He's been sentenced to 88 days in jail and 18 months probation.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:20.844
<v Casey>Bob Mackin, who covered it for the Tribune, described Russell's life as "off the rails" going into that sentencing.

00:02:22.044 --> 00:02:33.764
<v Riley>The B.C. Conservatives have named Cariboo-Chilcotin MLA Lorne Doerkson as interim leader of the party, and at the same time, three MLAs who had previously been removed from caucus are rejoining.

00:02:34.374 --> 00:02:37.118
<v Casey>So the shakeup resolves with our own MLA at the top of it.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:56.274
<v Riley>Doerkson's been the loudest Interior voice in that caucus on resource policy. He wrote a column just last week arguing the natural resource sector is in crisis — mills curtailing, tenure uncertainty, log supply shrinking. Now instead of just writing columns, he's setting caucus position on all of it.

00:02:57.598 --> 00:03:04.382
<v Casey>Whether that's a louder voice for the Cariboo specifically, or just a louder voice for the party generally, remains to be seen.

00:03:05.582 --> 00:03:22.198
<v Riley>Investigators are still working through what caused the wildfire near Summerland that destroyed more than a hundred structures. The Tribune's Brennan Phillips reports crews were still hosing down sections of the burn area as of Monday evening, and the cause hasn't been determined.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:32.414
<v Casey>No cause yet means no lessons yet — and that investigation is going to matter for every Cariboo community sitting inside beetle-killed or drought-stressed timber.

00:03:33.614 --> 00:04:05.038
<v Riley>Moving into stories that line up more with today's material — starting with something that sounds more like a video game story than an industry one. Modders have nearly halved the render time on AMD's BC-250 chip. That's the processor AMD originally built for PlayStation 5 cloud servers, and it's found a second life as a budget mining and gaming rig once the servers get decommissioned. A new FidelityFX driver cut 1440p upscaling time from 11.51 milliseconds down to 5.92.

00:04:06.268 --> 00:04:14.236
<v Casey>So somebody's getting a second act out of hardware that was supposed to be scrap — rescued by a hobbyist, not by anything the manufacturer built in.

00:04:15.436 --> 00:04:34.148
<v Riley>Design Academy Eindhoven put together an exhibition in Berlin called Behind Closed Doors — students reimagining the bathroom for a world with less water and fewer raw materials to spare. One project's a washbasin made of soap that dissolves the more you use it. Another uses human hair pressed into ceramic tile.

00:04:34.048 --> 00:04:38.312
<v Casey>A basin that disappears with use — that's either poetic or a plumbing nightmare.

00:04:39.423 --> 00:04:56.991
<v Riley>Possibly both. But the point underneath the show is designing deliberately for scarcity instead of designing for abundance and hoping the supply chain keeps up. Ranchers and mill towns here have been living that conversation for two decades without a gallery space in Berlin to put it in.

00:04:58.191 --> 00:05:24.015
<v Casey>A review published covering more than 153,000 adults found that calcium and vitamin D supplements do little to prevent fractures or falls for most older people, whether taken alone or together. Researchers are recommending people talk to an actual physician about bone density risk instead of defaulting to a pill bottle — advice that lands differently in a region where the nearest specialist appointment can mean a three-hour drive each way.

00:05:25.215 --> 00:05:42.919
<v Riley>Security researchers found a serious flaw in Flock's surveillance camera network — the same systems used for license plate recognition and increasingly marketed for rural property monitoring. One misconfigured device leaked one point six million images and twenty-seven thousand videos.

00:05:43.195 --> 00:05:44.235
<v Casey>From a single camera.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:06:06.834
<v Riley>From one. And TechRadar's reporting the company's internal reputation is bad enough right now that employees are reportedly job-hunting elsewhere. For anyone who's put a camera on a ranch gate thinking that feed was private, this is the reminder that privacy depends entirely on how the vendor built the back end, not on where the camera physically sits.

00:06:08.034 --> 00:06:20.674
<v Casey>A modelling report out from the NUS-Lancet Prime commission found nearly three million more lives could be saved globally in the next flu pandemic if vaccines were distributed according to population size instead of purchasing power.

00:06:21.864 --> 00:06:38.992
<v Riley>New Atlas also has a piece on research linking loss of the Y chromosome in blood cells to elevated cancer risk in men. It's early, but scientists think it could eventually work as a biomarker — the kind of finding that has a way of turning into a standard blood panel a decade later.

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:54.690
<v Casey>And Apple is finally letting customers file claims in its two hundred and fifty million dollar settlement over Siri failing to deliver the AI upgrades it had promised. If you're a U.S. iPhone owner with an eligible device, the claims window is open now.

00:06:54.590 --> 00:07:00.254
<v Riley>A quarter billion dollars because a voice assistant couldn't keep pace with its own press release.

00:07:01.454 --> 00:07:24.894
<v Casey>One more before the spotlight, from further afield but with an uncomfortable echo here. The board reviewing Los Angeles County's response to the Eaton and Palisades fires from earlier this year says numerous gaps remain in the recovery and repopulation process — permitting delays, confused communication with residents trying to rebuild, unclear timelines on when people could even go back and look at what was left.

00:07:26.337 --> 00:07:46.305
<v Riley>That's close to the same list of failure points we heard about after our own evacuation orders got downgraded last month — permitting, communication, and nobody quite sure who owns the follow-through once the cameras and the reporters leave. And that's a county with far more institutional capacity than most jurisdictions out here.

00:07:47.205 --> 00:08:04.933
<v Casey>Last one today, and it's a small story with a bigger shadow behind it. Hackaday's got a piece connecting rising housing costs to a slow shift toward bicycles as basic transportation rather than recreation — cargo bikes, e-bikes, the whole range, framed as an affordability response rather than a lifestyle choice.

00:08:06.292 --> 00:08:19.260
<v Riley>It's the kind of substitution that shows up on a gravel road here before it ever shows up in a government dataset — fuel costs and insurance pushing people toward whatever gets them to work without a car payment attached.

00:08:20.378 --> 00:08:33.498
<v Casey>And nobody in the Cariboo is tracking that shift formally. It just happens, one household budget at a time, and it never makes it into a labour force survey — which, funnily enough, is close to where today's Deep Dive is headed. Spotlight first, though.

00:08:34.898 --> 00:09:08.386
<v Riley>Today's World Tourism Day, and that's the Cariboo Chilcotin Coast Tourism Association's cue to remind everyone what's actually out the back door. They're the regional destination marketing organization covering the Cariboo, the Chilcotin, and the coast — promoting the outdoor recreation and the small rural businesses that keep a lot of these towns going outside the industrial economy. If a reason to get outside before the snow flies is what you need, or you run a business that depends on visitors finding you, they're at landwithoutlimits.com.

00:09:08.982 --> 00:09:18.806
<v Casey>Tourism doesn't usually get filed under working lands, but it's an economy running on exactly the same land base the mills and the ranches depend on. Different ledger, same land.

00:09:20.206 --> 00:09:25.606
<v Riley>When a mill curtails, somebody writes a headline. Nobody writes down what actually disappeared along with it.

00:09:26.228 --> 00:09:34.468
<v Casey>So does a stretch of working land still count as known, if the only thing recording it in detail is a satellite that never has to answer to the people standing on it?

00:09:34.788 --> 00:09:50.052
<v Riley>A regional pulp mill's curtailment near Quesnel is a good place to test that. It isn't a closure on paper — shifts stop, log supply gets redirected somewhere else in the system, and nobody publishes what the actual timber flow looks like six months after the fact.

00:09:50.686 --> 00:10:05.094
<v Casey>The province does track allowable annual cut through timber supply reviews, though. Those use satellite imagery, LiDAR flights, growth-and-yield models built off decades of forestry science. That's a real improvement over paper cruise data from thirty years ago.

00:10:05.774 --> 00:10:24.662
<v Riley>Sure, but a LiDAR flight gives you canopy height and stand density from four hundred kilometres up. It doesn't tell you which drainage had root rot last year, or which block a faller refused to touch because the ground was unstable after a burn. That knowledge lived in the people who walked the cruise lines on foot.

00:10:25.247 --> 00:10:43.319
<v Casey>Some of whom are in their sixties and seventies now. So when a curtailing mill lays off its most experienced cruisers and fallers first — because they're the highest-paid — the region doesn't just lose a paycheque. It loses the only people who actually knew what was standing out there beyond what a sensor can register.

00:10:43.819 --> 00:10:57.195
<v Riley>That's a sharper version of the point than "the sector is in crisis." Doerkson has made that argument publicly, and he's got real political weight behind it now as interim leader. But a statement of concern isn't an inventory.

00:10:57.753 --> 00:11:13.241
<v Casey>The Red Rock inspection station turns into a strange kind of data point here. Nine point two million dollars to rebuild a commercial vehicle inspection station on Highway 97 south of Prince George, after a fire took the original one down in December of 2024.

00:11:13.421 --> 00:11:14.973
<v Riley>A truck-weighing station.

00:11:15.558 --> 00:11:28.550
<v Casey>But every log truck moving through that station gets weighed and logged — a running measure of how much timber is actually leaving the land base, closer to real time than a timber supply review that runs on a five- or ten-year cycle.

00:11:29.109 --> 00:11:44.613
<v Riley>Who actually gets that data, though? Weigh station records go to the Ministry of Transportation for load safety and compliance. They don't go anywhere near a public dashboard someone in Quesnel or Williams Lake could check against the mill curtailment numbers.

00:11:45.262 --> 00:12:08.566
<v Casey>That's a real gap. The infrastructure exists. Nobody's built the bridge from that infrastructure to something a community could use — and that's the pattern across the whole sector. The instruments keep getting sharper while the guest list for who's allowed to read them stays exactly the same: province, licensee, sometimes a consultant hired by the licensee. Never the town whose mill just curtailed a hundred jobs.

00:12:09.172 --> 00:12:24.564
<v Riley>Except that's not the whole picture. There are Nation-held forestry ventures operating on tenure they hold directly, where the people deciding what gets harvested are the same people who live with whatever the consequences turn out to be — sensor data or not.

00:12:25.183 --> 00:12:47.143
<v Casey>That's a different structure, and a better one. But it's a handful of Nation-held operations set against a province-wide inventory system built for company licensees. It doesn't close the information gap for the cruisers who just got laid off, and it doesn't help a rancher trying to figure out whether the range assessment on their tenure reflects this year's grass or a model from four years ago.

00:12:47.643 --> 00:13:03.011
<v Riley>Ranching's got its own version of the same problem. Range health assessments on Crown grazing tenure happen on a rotation, sometimes years apart, while the rancher standing on that land every single day could tell you within a season if a paddock's degrading.

00:13:03.699 --> 00:13:17.011
<v Casey>And when the assessment finally arrives and doesn't match what the rancher's actually been seeing, there's no formal channel to reconcile the two. The province's record wins by default, because it's the province's tenure and the province's paperwork.

00:13:17.619 --> 00:13:32.491
<v Riley>Which brings us to the real question underneath all of this — not whether better sensors exist, they clearly do, but whether local knowledge gets any standing next to them when a tenure decision, a curtailment, or a range assessment actually gets made.

00:13:33.083 --> 00:13:42.219
<v Casey>Standing's the right word for it. Right now the ground-truth side of that equation retires, gets laid off, or just ages out, and nothing formal steps in to replace it.

00:13:42.619 --> 00:13:47.939
<v Riley>So what would replacing it actually look like? Not a grant program — something structural.

00:13:48.584 --> 00:14:11.432
<v Casey>Start smaller than a policy fix. What if a community forest board, or a producer association, kept its own running log — harvest volumes, range conditions, mill throughput — and cross-checked it against whatever the province publishes? Some watershed groups already track stream flow independently of the Ministry of Environment, so there's precedent for a local record running parallel to the official one.

00:14:11.941 --> 00:14:25.733
<v Riley>That already exists in miniature. Some B.C. community forests publish annual reports with harvest and silviculture data specific to their own tenure. It's public, it's local, and it predates this whole LiDAR conversation by years.

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:38.498
<v Casey>Then the honest question is why that model hasn't spread across the licensee-held tenure that covers most of the Cariboo's working forest. Community forests are a small fraction of the total land base out here.

00:14:39.065 --> 00:14:53.657
<v Riley>Because the tenure holder has no legal obligation to publish anything beyond what the Forest Act requires, and the Forest Act doesn't require ground-truth reconciliation with anyone local. That's a regulatory gap, not a technology gap.

00:14:54.157 --> 00:15:07.757
<v Casey>Which means any real fix runs through the next tenure renewal cycle. Make local reporting a condition of holding the tenure, the same way Impact Benefit Agreements get attached to a permit instead of to whoever happens to own the company that quarter.

00:15:08.355 --> 00:15:19.419
<v Riley>Consistent with what we've said about mining tenure before — tie the obligation to the permit, not the ownership structure sitting on top of it, because ownership changes and permits don't.

00:15:20.012 --> 00:15:28.572
<v Casey>It's a slow lever, though. Tenure renewals run on decade-long cycles, and none of the laid-off cruisers get their jobs back before the next one comes due.

00:15:29.184 --> 00:15:42.032
<v Riley>No argument there. But it's a lever that survives a curtailment, a corporate sale, or a change in provincial government, which none of the sensor infrastructure does on its own — LiDAR data still belongs to whoever commissioned the flight.

00:15:42.631 --> 00:15:57.751
<v Casey>There's a smaller version of this too — something a town could try without waiting on Victoria at all. A local forest liaison committee, made up of retired fallers and cruisers, meeting a couple times a year to compare notes against whatever the licensee's latest cut plan says.

00:15:58.317 --> 00:16:06.525
<v Riley>That's cheap. It doesn't require legislative change, just a room and a willingness from the licensee to actually show up and listen.

00:16:07.155 --> 00:16:17.419
<v Casey>The willingness part is the whole obstacle. Nothing compels a licensee to sit in that room, and nothing compels the province to treat what comes out of it as anything more than a courtesy.

00:16:17.819 --> 00:16:35.635
<v Riley>Which is the honest place to leave it. The tools for building that kind of standing exist on paper — tenure conditions, community forest reporting, even something as informal as a liaison committee. Whether any of it gets picked up before the next round of retirements is a separate question.

00:16:36.254 --> 00:16:50.678
<v Casey>And it's the one worth tracking, rather than the one that gets a press release. The forest gets recorded in more detail every single year, and the people who actually know it best still have no formal channel to argue with the record. That part hasn't moved.

00:16:51.478 --> 00:17:10.350
<v Riley>If you've sat through a tenure review hearing, or you're on a community forest board already doing some version of this, tell us what a working reconciliation between local knowledge and the official record actually looks like — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. That's the kind of proof this whole conversation is waiting on.

00:17:11.008 --> 00:17:17.096
<v Casey>And if it doesn't exist anywhere yet, that's worth knowing too. Thanks for spending part of your morning with us.

00:17:17.401 --> 00:17:18.601
<v Riley>Have a great rest of your day.

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:19.300
<v Casey>Catch you tomorrow.